WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.427 --> 00:00:02.260
- Hi everyone.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.617
Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.617 --> 00:00:06.387
Welcome to the homeroom live stream.

00:00:06.387 --> 00:00:08.107
Actually, I think this
is the first of the year.

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:10.727
Hopefully everyone had a good New Year's,

00:00:10.727 --> 00:00:14.257
considering the circumstances
and is enjoying 2021,

00:00:14.257 --> 00:00:15.767
given the circumstances.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:18.437
We have a very exciting show today.

00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:20.867
I think someone who needs
really no introduction,

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:23.247
but Melinda Gates, co-chair

00:00:23.247 --> 00:00:25.127
of the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation

00:00:25.127 --> 00:00:27.407
and author of Moment of Lift:

00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:29.707
How Empowering Women Changes the World.

00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:31.387
So start thinking of questions

00:00:31.387 --> 00:00:33.157
that you could put in
on YouTube and Facebook.

00:00:33.157 --> 00:00:35.857
We'll try to get to as many of
those questions as possible.

00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:37.647
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:37.647 --> 00:00:39.427
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:41.287
A reminder that Khan Academy,

00:00:41.287 --> 00:00:43.187
we are a not-for-profit organization

00:00:43.187 --> 00:00:45.197
that's funded through
philanthropic donations

00:00:45.197 --> 00:00:46.707
from folks like yourself.

00:00:46.707 --> 00:00:48.367
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.177
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:56.217
And donations at all levels
really make a huge difference.

00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:59.354
I also want to make a shout
out to several organizations

00:00:59.354 --> 00:01:01.617
that stepped up when they realized

00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:03.567
that the world was
depending on Khan Academy

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.067
even more during COVID, so special thanks

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.247
to Bank of America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:11.597
Novartis, Fastly, and General Motors,

00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:12.957
but we continue to need more help.

00:01:12.957 --> 00:01:15.347
So anything you can do
is very much appreciated.

00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:17.547
And last but not least,
want to remind everyone

00:01:17.547 --> 00:01:20.937
that you can get a version
of this live stream

00:01:20.937 --> 00:01:23.277
in podcast form, wherever
you get your podcasts

00:01:23.277 --> 00:01:26.477
at Homeroom with Sal the podcast.

00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:31.477
With that, I'm so excited
to introduce Melinda Gates,

00:01:31.687 --> 00:01:34.017
author of Moment of Lift:

00:01:34.017 --> 00:01:37.847
How Empowering Women Changes
the World, and co-chair

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:39.897
of the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:43.047
Melinda, great to see you.

00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:43.880
- Hi Sal.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.860
Thanks for having me.

00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:49.547
- Well, there's so much
that I could talk about,

00:01:49.547 --> 00:01:50.917
and you cover a lot of this in your book

00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:52.747
and there's a lot of work that
you've done beyond the book.

00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:54.710
Maybe a good place to start
would be with the book.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:58.067
Why did you decide that now is
a good time to write a book?

00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:00.000
And what are your hopes for it?

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.967
- Well, I wrote this book
and introduced it in 2019,

00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:08.057
and now we're coming out
just now with the paperback.

00:02:08.057 --> 00:02:11.417
And I wrote it at the time
because I had been lucky enough

00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:13.687
to travel the world
because of the Foundation.

00:02:13.687 --> 00:02:18.347
And I'd met so many women who
had really animated my life

00:02:18.347 --> 00:02:20.437
and called me to action.

00:02:20.437 --> 00:02:22.867
And I felt if I could tell their stories

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:25.337
it might call the world to action.

00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:28.327
And when I wrote this book,
I think we, as a community

00:02:28.327 --> 00:02:31.707
thought we were on our way
towards gender equality.

00:02:31.707 --> 00:02:34.067
And now here we are in
the middle of a pandemic

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:38.047
in early 2021 and women
have really been set back

00:02:38.047 --> 00:02:40.847
in the United States
and all over the globe.

00:02:40.847 --> 00:02:44.610
So we have all a lot
more work ahead of us.

00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:48.027
- And what's been surprising, you know,

00:02:48.027 --> 00:02:50.307
when I saw the book and
I started reading it,

00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:53.737
I expected to hear a lot of
the stories of your travels,

00:02:53.737 --> 00:02:55.597
the work that y'all have been
doing with the Foundation.

00:02:55.597 --> 00:02:56.957
And there's definitely that,

00:02:56.957 --> 00:02:57.987
but what's been really interesting,

00:02:57.987 --> 00:02:59.887
it's also a very personal book where you

00:02:59.887 --> 00:03:02.887
kind of thread in your own experience

00:03:02.887 --> 00:03:07.087
trying to balance the
different pulls on your time

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:09.627
from trying to be a great mother,

00:03:09.627 --> 00:03:12.247
trying to have a great
career at the same time,

00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:14.687
and kind of relating that to, you know,

00:03:14.687 --> 00:03:17.137
a general theme of women around the world

00:03:17.137 --> 00:03:20.657
have all of these things that
they're trying to balance.

00:03:20.657 --> 00:03:23.057
- Yes, I thought it was
important to include

00:03:23.057 --> 00:03:24.447
my personal story.

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:28.127
I mean, to begin with,
you know, I do live a life

00:03:28.127 --> 00:03:30.707
of extreme privilege now, but growing up,

00:03:30.707 --> 00:03:33.297
I grew up in a very middle-class family

00:03:33.297 --> 00:03:34.787
in the United States.

00:03:34.787 --> 00:03:36.937
And I think no matter whether you're in

00:03:36.937 --> 00:03:40.067
a low income country, middle
income or high income,

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:43.507
women face a lot in their lives

00:03:43.507 --> 00:03:46.107
that we just don't talk about.

00:03:46.107 --> 00:03:49.727
And I thought it was important
to talk about my own journey

00:03:49.727 --> 00:03:52.967
and my own struggles with
balancing being a mom,

00:03:52.967 --> 00:03:57.447
which I absolutely wanted to
be and am, and a working woman.

00:03:57.447 --> 00:04:01.737
And I tried to then highlight
how that also is similar

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:03.927
for so many women around the globe

00:04:03.927 --> 00:04:06.327
who don't even have the
resources that I have

00:04:06.327 --> 00:04:08.750
to get some extra support and help.

00:04:10.277 --> 00:04:11.777
- So I want to go into
actually both of those,

00:04:11.777 --> 00:04:13.227
and I'm sure there'll be a
lot of questions to that,

00:04:13.227 --> 00:04:16.454
but first when we're
talking about helping women

00:04:16.454 --> 00:04:17.287
around the world, and obviously there's

00:04:17.287 --> 00:04:19.087
the pre-COVID context in
which you wrote the book,

00:04:19.087 --> 00:04:21.197
and then what you're able to see both

00:04:21.197 --> 00:04:23.857
in the broader kind of, you
could say industrializing world

00:04:23.857 --> 00:04:26.207
and in the in the industrialized world,

00:04:26.207 --> 00:04:28.027
what COVID stresses or putting on,

00:04:28.027 --> 00:04:30.167
what do you see as, you know,

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.177
if you want people to walk away

00:04:32.177 --> 00:04:34.627
with the handful of things that are

00:04:34.627 --> 00:04:36.227
the real challenges facing women

00:04:36.227 --> 00:04:38.877
that we really have to
address, what are they to you?

00:04:39.787 --> 00:04:43.339
- They are, to me, the
barriers that hold women back

00:04:43.339 --> 00:04:46.387
in society that we so
often don't talk about,

00:04:46.387 --> 00:04:49.967
harassment and abuse, whether
at home or in the workplace.

00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:52.097
The other big barrier, quite frankly,

00:04:52.097 --> 00:04:57.097
is this child care work, this
unpaid labor that women do

00:04:57.587 --> 00:04:59.877
in their homes every single day.

00:04:59.877 --> 00:05:01.827
No matter where you are in the world,

00:05:01.827 --> 00:05:05.447
women do more unpaid
work than their husband

00:05:05.447 --> 00:05:06.837
or their spouse does.

00:05:06.837 --> 00:05:11.077
And that keeps them often from
being fully in the workforce

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:12.677
and being fully present.

00:05:12.677 --> 00:05:16.807
And then there are many things
we can do to accelerate women

00:05:16.807 --> 00:05:19.707
in key, key industries,
making sure we get money

00:05:19.707 --> 00:05:22.517
in their hands, accelerate
them in finance,

00:05:22.517 --> 00:05:25.207
accelerate them in the tech
sector in the United States,

00:05:25.207 --> 00:05:28.767
accelerate them in policy
making and political positions,

00:05:28.767 --> 00:05:33.767
because until you have women
with a seat at the table

00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:37.587
using their full voice and
decision-making authority,

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:40.117
we will never have sound policies

00:05:40.117 --> 00:05:42.977
that benefit all of society.

00:05:42.977 --> 00:05:45.627
And those are imperatives for us

00:05:45.627 --> 00:05:48.630
if we're going to really
move forward as a world.

00:05:50.187 --> 00:05:51.147
- You know, there's a lot in that.

00:05:51.147 --> 00:05:53.537
You know, there's a fairness argument,

00:05:53.537 --> 00:05:54.777
which is a very strong one.

00:05:54.777 --> 00:05:55.997
There's the argument you just made,

00:05:55.997 --> 00:05:58.957
which is this argument around,
you really need a diversity

00:05:58.957 --> 00:06:01.077
of viewpoints around the
decision-making table,

00:06:01.077 --> 00:06:02.667
especially if it's going to affect people

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:05.097
of different viewpoints,

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.507
but you know, your book you refer to,

00:06:06.507 --> 00:06:08.217
and this definitely
struck a chord with me,

00:06:08.217 --> 00:06:10.257
you talk about the study in Bangladesh,

00:06:10.257 --> 00:06:12.177
which is where my family
originally comes from,

00:06:12.177 --> 00:06:14.227
where they're able to
actually to some degree

00:06:14.227 --> 00:06:18.447
run an experiment where women
were more in the driver's seat

00:06:18.447 --> 00:06:22.037
of things like family
planning and what that led to.

00:06:22.037 --> 00:06:24.317
It didn't just lead to better
outcomes for those women.

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:25.607
If you could talk a little bit about that,

00:06:25.607 --> 00:06:28.837
because I think that's a
very super poignant example

00:06:28.837 --> 00:06:31.200
for why it's so important
to invest in women.

00:06:32.127 --> 00:06:33.667
- Yeah, thank you for that question, Sal,

00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:37.327
because I think so often
we don't talk about

00:06:37.327 --> 00:06:39.307
family planning or contraceptives.

00:06:39.307 --> 00:06:42.587
And yet when you look
in the United States,

00:06:42.587 --> 00:06:46.637
what has allowed women to go to college

00:06:46.637 --> 00:06:50.187
and to get a degree and
to work in the workforce?

00:06:50.187 --> 00:06:54.957
It's being able to decide
when, whether and how often

00:06:54.957 --> 00:06:56.297
to have children.

00:06:56.297 --> 00:06:59.297
So in Bangladesh, as you
referenced in the 1970s

00:06:59.297 --> 00:07:02.707
the longest global health
study that's ever been run

00:07:02.707 --> 00:07:04.477
were two different sets of villages,

00:07:04.477 --> 00:07:08.317
where one set had full
access to contraceptives.

00:07:08.317 --> 00:07:10.337
Women were educated about their bodies,

00:07:10.337 --> 00:07:12.337
all types of methods of contraceptives,

00:07:12.337 --> 00:07:13.487
and then they used them,

00:07:13.487 --> 00:07:16.057
and other villages that
didn't have access.

00:07:16.057 --> 00:07:18.511
And what they found over this long study

00:07:18.511 --> 00:07:21.647
was that in the villages
where contraceptives

00:07:21.647 --> 00:07:24.357
were readily available and used,

00:07:24.357 --> 00:07:28.257
the families were healthier,
the children were healthier,

00:07:28.257 --> 00:07:30.447
the children were better educated,

00:07:30.447 --> 00:07:33.777
and the families overall were wealthier.

00:07:33.777 --> 00:07:37.647
And that's because the moms
and dads could space the births

00:07:37.647 --> 00:07:41.987
of their children and feed
and educate each one of them

00:07:41.987 --> 00:07:44.507
and start down that path to prosperity

00:07:44.507 --> 00:07:46.807
before they chose to have another child.

00:07:46.807 --> 00:07:49.677
And that it makes a profound difference

00:07:49.677 --> 00:07:52.717
in terms of unlocking the cycle of poverty

00:07:52.717 --> 00:07:54.830
for a woman and for her family.

00:07:56.387 --> 00:07:57.267
- Well, you talk about that.

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:58.487
I mean, when you point it out,

00:07:58.487 --> 00:08:00.307
it's something we so take for granted.

00:08:00.307 --> 00:08:02.417
You mention in the book how you were able

00:08:02.417 --> 00:08:03.877
to essentially plan things out.

00:08:03.877 --> 00:08:06.717
It made me reflect on
my wife and my journey

00:08:06.717 --> 00:08:09.827
where you know, my wife also,
she's more educated than I am,

00:08:09.827 --> 00:08:12.737
she went to med school and
that for our first child,

00:08:12.737 --> 00:08:14.577
we kind of said, okay, there's that year

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:16.487
right after residency, before you start.

00:08:16.487 --> 00:08:18.368
Okay, let's have our first child then.

00:08:18.368 --> 00:08:21.097
And that ability, which
we take for granted

00:08:21.097 --> 00:08:22.827
is not something that's commonplace

00:08:22.827 --> 00:08:24.397
for most of human history,
and unfortunately,

00:08:24.397 --> 00:08:26.847
in a lot of part of the
world is still not expected.

00:08:26.847 --> 00:08:29.180
And so it's really, when
you're able to space it out,

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:30.877
a family can have resources,

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:32.887
they can invest in their children.

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:35.327
And then obviously I can imagine
that has follow-on effects.

00:08:35.327 --> 00:08:37.360
Are there other, you know,

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.651
it seems like that's
one of the top things,

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:40.520
just the ability to have control

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.157
and be able to space things out

00:08:42.157 --> 00:08:43.977
and make sure that the resources.

00:08:43.977 --> 00:08:45.917
Are there other big things that you think

00:08:45.917 --> 00:08:48.617
folks should know about, and, you know,

00:08:48.617 --> 00:08:50.777
folks being everyone listening,
people in government,

00:08:50.777 --> 00:08:52.437
people in the philanthropic sector,

00:08:52.437 --> 00:08:54.367
that are high leverage
points for empowering,

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:56.787
not just women, but because
you're empowering them,

00:08:56.787 --> 00:08:59.347
the communities that
they're they're part of?

00:08:59.347 --> 00:09:02.887
- Well, certainly two things
I would say: educating a girl.

00:09:02.887 --> 00:09:07.567
When a girl has access to
school and to a good education,

00:09:07.567 --> 00:09:11.337
she makes completely different
decisions about her life.

00:09:11.337 --> 00:09:14.587
And when she has access to those new ideas

00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:18.257
and that new thinking,
she also delays, usually,

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:21.447
when she gets married and
when she has children.

00:09:21.447 --> 00:09:24.497
That again, has a profound
effect on a community,

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:26.507
not just at a community level,

00:09:26.507 --> 00:09:29.037
but at a regional level
and at a country level.

00:09:29.037 --> 00:09:32.357
And it's why finally, in the last 10 years

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:34.227
so many presidents and prime ministers

00:09:34.227 --> 00:09:36.607
actually finally show up at the UN

00:09:36.607 --> 00:09:38.997
and talk about girls' education,

00:09:38.997 --> 00:09:41.607
getting girls into school and keeping them

00:09:41.607 --> 00:09:44.947
all the way through at
least secondary school.

00:09:44.947 --> 00:09:48.517
So that is a really important
factor, is girls education,

00:09:48.517 --> 00:09:49.627
just like boys.

00:09:49.627 --> 00:09:52.837
And the other one is
getting economic means

00:09:52.837 --> 00:09:54.277
in a woman's hand.

00:09:54.277 --> 00:09:57.877
So all over the world, one of
the things that we now have,

00:09:57.877 --> 00:09:59.477
of course, are phones.

00:09:59.477 --> 00:10:01.327
and even if you don't have a smartphone,

00:10:01.327 --> 00:10:04.262
but you have access to
an old plastic phone,

00:10:04.262 --> 00:10:06.367
there are governments all over the world,

00:10:06.367 --> 00:10:10.037
India, Bangladesh, many
others, Tanzania, Kenya,

00:10:10.037 --> 00:10:12.577
where they're making
sure that women and men

00:10:12.577 --> 00:10:16.677
can save a dollar a day,
$2 a day on their phones.

00:10:16.677 --> 00:10:18.537
So they actually have a bank account,

00:10:18.537 --> 00:10:21.127
so that when the school
fees come due for the child,

00:10:21.127 --> 00:10:23.327
or there's a health shock in the family,

00:10:23.327 --> 00:10:25.377
or they need to purchase food

00:10:25.377 --> 00:10:28.377
the woman has money of her own,

00:10:28.377 --> 00:10:30.477
that is not her husband's money.

00:10:30.477 --> 00:10:32.147
And that is vitally important,

00:10:32.147 --> 00:10:35.227
because it empowers her in the family.

00:10:35.227 --> 00:10:37.337
And she is often the one who decides

00:10:37.337 --> 00:10:39.237
who's going to eat
first, second, and third,

00:10:39.237 --> 00:10:41.550
or who's going to go
to the medical facility

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.997
or who's going to help pay for the tuition

00:10:43.997 --> 00:10:45.707
when it comes due at the school.

00:10:45.707 --> 00:10:48.787
So a woman, when she
has money in her hands,

00:10:48.787 --> 00:10:51.227
she can get out of an abusive situation.

00:10:51.227 --> 00:10:53.467
She can invest in a small business.

00:10:53.467 --> 00:10:55.247
She can make different decisions

00:10:55.247 --> 00:10:56.877
for herself and her children.

00:10:56.877 --> 00:11:00.637
Money is power and it makes a difference

00:11:00.637 --> 00:11:03.350
for women's own power and influence.

00:11:04.887 --> 00:11:05.967
- It makes a ton of sense.

00:11:05.967 --> 00:11:08.323
You know, it leads to a natural question,

00:11:08.323 --> 00:11:09.814
When I read some of the stats, the studies

00:11:09.814 --> 00:11:12.217
on these various dimensions,
it seems like a no-brainer

00:11:12.217 --> 00:11:13.907
that more of the world
should be focused on this,

00:11:13.907 --> 00:11:15.472
should be investing.

00:11:15.472 --> 00:11:16.305
I know there's been a lot of progress

00:11:16.305 --> 00:11:17.937
over the last several decades,

00:11:17.937 --> 00:11:19.927
but it also makes me
wonder why weren't people

00:11:19.927 --> 00:11:21.217
already doing that?

00:11:21.217 --> 00:11:24.787
You know, the role of why
haven't governments, you know,

00:11:24.787 --> 00:11:26.907
why has there had to been such an emphasis

00:11:26.907 --> 00:11:28.227
on the philanthropic sector,

00:11:28.227 --> 00:11:29.567
and then maybe take the other side.

00:11:29.567 --> 00:11:31.813
You in the book mentioned when
you first met Hans Rosling,

00:11:31.813 --> 00:11:34.297
famous data visualizer.

00:11:34.297 --> 00:11:35.457
How he was skeptical.

00:11:35.457 --> 00:11:36.607
I think he said something like,

00:11:36.607 --> 00:11:38.957
American billionaires
are gonna mess things up.

00:11:38.957 --> 00:11:42.157
And you hear that more and
more in the public narrative

00:11:42.157 --> 00:11:44.597
of people saying, well
philanthropy sounds good,

00:11:44.597 --> 00:11:47.377
but we're not so sure or
maybe this is too much power

00:11:47.377 --> 00:11:49.047
in the hands of a few people.

00:11:49.047 --> 00:11:50.617
Where do you see that balance?

00:11:50.617 --> 00:11:51.707
Where's the role of philanthropy

00:11:51.707 --> 00:11:56.017
and what's your sense of
how you make people realize

00:11:56.017 --> 00:11:58.667
that there's actually a lot of value here?

00:11:58.667 --> 00:12:00.947
- So let me start with
the second question,

00:12:00.947 --> 00:12:02.407
which is philanthropy's role.

00:12:02.407 --> 00:12:05.947
I think philanthropy does its best

00:12:05.947 --> 00:12:09.777
when it understands that
it is part of an ecosystem.

00:12:09.777 --> 00:12:13.977
What you need is philanthropy, government,

00:12:13.977 --> 00:12:18.447
private sector, that is
businesses, civil society,

00:12:18.447 --> 00:12:23.347
all working together to
benefit the world and society.

00:12:23.347 --> 00:12:26.507
What can philanthropic money do

00:12:26.507 --> 00:12:28.467
that some of the others can't?

00:12:28.467 --> 00:12:31.427
They can take risks
where there is no market,

00:12:31.427 --> 00:12:33.297
so a business won't enter it,

00:12:33.297 --> 00:12:36.077
or where the government
doesn't want to take the risk,

00:12:36.077 --> 00:12:37.177
with the citizens' money.

00:12:37.177 --> 00:12:38.867
It can try new things.

00:12:38.867 --> 00:12:40.927
It can further new ideas.

00:12:40.927 --> 00:12:43.207
And if those things prove out,

00:12:43.207 --> 00:12:45.517
then it's up to government to fund them

00:12:45.517 --> 00:12:48.027
and to spread those ideas.

00:12:48.027 --> 00:12:50.637
So to your question though,
the earlier one was,

00:12:50.637 --> 00:12:52.473
why was it a decade ago when, you know,

00:12:52.473 --> 00:12:55.507
15 years ago I happened to be at the UN

00:12:55.507 --> 00:12:57.227
or other places with other global leaders,

00:12:57.227 --> 00:12:59.117
no one talked about girls, first of all.

00:12:59.117 --> 00:13:01.037
Girls were not on the agenda.

00:13:01.037 --> 00:13:02.417
Women were only on the agenda

00:13:02.417 --> 00:13:04.897
when it came to their maternal health.

00:13:04.897 --> 00:13:06.507
prime ministers and presidents didn't talk

00:13:06.507 --> 00:13:08.897
about women and girls, other than that.

00:13:08.897 --> 00:13:10.277
Well, why is that?

00:13:10.277 --> 00:13:13.934
It's because we run in
a society where males

00:13:13.934 --> 00:13:16.087
run the top of society.

00:13:16.087 --> 00:13:19.137
They run the political
bodies, they run quite often

00:13:19.137 --> 00:13:22.877
the social media platforms,
they run the newspapers.

00:13:22.877 --> 00:13:27.527
And so you have to make
sure that you actually make

00:13:27.527 --> 00:13:31.277
extra investments to help women rise up

00:13:31.277 --> 00:13:33.577
into those positions of power,

00:13:33.577 --> 00:13:36.327
because if you don't have a diverse

00:13:36.327 --> 00:13:38.077
slate of people at the table,

00:13:38.077 --> 00:13:42.997
that is in our own country
people of color, women and men,

00:13:42.997 --> 00:13:45.887
you then don't get a diverse
representation of society,

00:13:45.887 --> 00:13:49.677
and so you make government
policy based on what you know,

00:13:49.677 --> 00:13:53.527
but if that comes from say
a group of just white men,

00:13:53.527 --> 00:13:57.117
they don't see the full view of society

00:13:57.117 --> 00:13:59.087
that a woman or a person of color

00:13:59.087 --> 00:14:01.497
with their lived
experience would have seen.

00:14:01.497 --> 00:14:04.627
And thus you don't build
into society the places

00:14:04.627 --> 00:14:07.027
that will unlock opportunities

00:14:07.027 --> 00:14:08.957
for women and people of color.

00:14:08.957 --> 00:14:11.607
And that's why I am such a big proponent

00:14:11.607 --> 00:14:14.387
of making sure we create pathways

00:14:14.387 --> 00:14:16.847
for women into the key, key industries,

00:14:16.847 --> 00:14:20.767
such as politics or finance or tech,

00:14:20.767 --> 00:14:24.507
because of how pervasive
and how much those areas

00:14:24.507 --> 00:14:26.640
run our society and our globe.

00:14:28.327 --> 00:14:29.367
- I couldn't agree more.

00:14:29.367 --> 00:14:31.167
And, you know, the question I asked,

00:14:33.677 --> 00:14:36.207
obviously, in full
disclosure, many folks know

00:14:36.207 --> 00:14:39.397
your foundation has been,
one of kind of the founding

00:14:39.397 --> 00:14:41.047
sponsors of Khan Academy.

00:14:41.047 --> 00:14:42.897
So obviously, I have some biases here

00:14:42.897 --> 00:14:45.137
that I should have a whole disclaimer for,

00:14:45.137 --> 00:14:46.737
but Khan Academy would not exist

00:14:46.737 --> 00:14:48.367
without the philanthropic sector.

00:14:48.367 --> 00:14:50.877
And it absolutely, even when
my friends were skeptical,

00:14:50.877 --> 00:14:52.247
why does Khan Academy even exist?

00:14:52.247 --> 00:14:53.947
Shouldn't it be for profit or
shouldn't it be government?

00:14:53.947 --> 00:14:57.627
It's exactly in that zone
where it needs to move fast

00:14:57.627 --> 00:14:59.937
and innovate, but it
needs to operate on values

00:14:59.937 --> 00:15:02.877
that may be market forces,
which I'm normally a big fan of,

00:15:02.877 --> 00:15:04.577
might not lead to the outcomes we want.

00:15:04.577 --> 00:15:05.827
And that's just a small example,

00:15:05.827 --> 00:15:07.817
but some of the work that
y'all have been doing

00:15:07.817 --> 00:15:12.777
with malaria, with polio, with diarrhea,

00:15:12.777 --> 00:15:14.647
some of these things that
shouldn't be killing anyone

00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:16.847
for the last several decades,
were killing thousands

00:15:16.847 --> 00:15:18.901
or in some cases, millions of people.

00:15:18.901 --> 00:15:22.197
I mean, both you and Bill,
I think are very humble

00:15:22.197 --> 00:15:24.797
about how many millions,
literally millions

00:15:24.797 --> 00:15:27.297
of lives you saved and
arguably it should be

00:15:27.297 --> 00:15:30.207
part of government, but without
the philanthropic sector

00:15:30.207 --> 00:15:32.997
there I don't think, these
aren't vanity projects.

00:15:32.997 --> 00:15:35.155
I don't think any of
that would have happened.

00:15:35.155 --> 00:15:36.887
You know, one question,

00:15:36.887 --> 00:15:39.237
a lot of questions coming
in on social media,

00:15:39.237 --> 00:15:40.070
I'll get to some of them.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:44.117
I've been using my privilege
to take all my questions,

00:15:44.117 --> 00:15:46.877
but this question, is
related to that last point.

00:15:46.877 --> 00:15:49.947
It's from YouTube, from chillax.

00:15:49.947 --> 00:15:52.747
What do you think you have achieved

00:15:52.747 --> 00:15:54.737
by working and collaborating with women

00:15:54.737 --> 00:15:55.757
in different countries?

00:15:55.757 --> 00:15:57.197
And, you know, I do wonder that.

00:15:57.197 --> 00:15:59.047
You and Bill, you go on
a lot of these trips,

00:15:59.047 --> 00:16:01.297
you go on a lot of trips,
you visit villages.

00:16:01.297 --> 00:16:04.657
You're really in places that
many of us have never been to.

00:16:04.657 --> 00:16:05.947
My family is from Bangladesh.

00:16:05.947 --> 00:16:07.857
I think you've gone into more

00:16:07.857 --> 00:16:10.577
of the real rural villages
of Bangladesh than I,

00:16:10.577 --> 00:16:14.042
or many members of my
family have ever been to.

00:16:14.042 --> 00:16:15.307
What do you learn from those trips

00:16:15.307 --> 00:16:17.390
that you might not have
appreciated before?

00:16:18.247 --> 00:16:21.647
- Well, I think it's fundamental to travel

00:16:21.647 --> 00:16:24.447
and to listen and to learn,

00:16:24.447 --> 00:16:26.937
because I think, we could come in

00:16:26.937 --> 00:16:28.977
with our Western point of view of,

00:16:28.977 --> 00:16:31.017
Oh, you need this for your child,

00:16:31.017 --> 00:16:32.337
or this is the way to do things

00:16:32.337 --> 00:16:34.877
because we do it that
way in the US or Europe.

00:16:34.877 --> 00:16:36.297
When in fact you listen to the locals,

00:16:36.297 --> 00:16:39.207
they have very good reasons
for doing what they do,

00:16:39.207 --> 00:16:43.087
and they have seen more death
and disability than we have.

00:16:43.087 --> 00:16:44.807
They can tell you what might work

00:16:44.807 --> 00:16:47.727
or what the barriers
are that hold them back.

00:16:47.727 --> 00:16:50.637
You can come in for instance,
with the very best vaccine

00:16:50.637 --> 00:16:52.987
or new tool, let's say for malaria,

00:16:52.987 --> 00:16:55.757
but if the village elder
who makes the decision

00:16:55.757 --> 00:16:58.167
won't let that tool be taken up,

00:16:58.167 --> 00:17:00.087
it's going to go nowhere.

00:17:00.087 --> 00:17:02.547
And so for women, what I have learned

00:17:02.547 --> 00:17:06.077
is that you need to go in, sit
down with them in their homes

00:17:06.077 --> 00:17:08.347
and where they gather
in the village market

00:17:08.347 --> 00:17:12.457
or on a local mat, talk
with them, and the men,

00:17:12.457 --> 00:17:15.247
eventually let the men wander
away back to the fields

00:17:15.247 --> 00:17:17.607
or to their business, and then you hear

00:17:17.607 --> 00:17:19.947
the truth of women's lives.

00:17:19.947 --> 00:17:21.677
And you hear what they're up against,

00:17:21.677 --> 00:17:23.747
what lengths they will go to,

00:17:23.747 --> 00:17:27.067
how they do take their meager
means and feed their children,

00:17:27.067 --> 00:17:30.147
and you learn that women
are incredibly resilient.

00:17:30.147 --> 00:17:32.307
And so one of the things
I've been fortunate enough

00:17:32.307 --> 00:17:34.027
to be involved or help lead

00:17:34.027 --> 00:17:36.037
is this effort on family planning

00:17:36.037 --> 00:17:38.987
because so many women have told me,

00:17:38.987 --> 00:17:40.787
I've heard of those tools.

00:17:40.787 --> 00:17:42.947
We used to have them in my village.

00:17:42.947 --> 00:17:43.937
I don't have them now.

00:17:43.937 --> 00:17:45.147
And I have six children.

00:17:45.147 --> 00:17:46.517
I cannot have a seventh.

00:17:46.517 --> 00:17:48.417
I cannot feed these children.

00:17:48.417 --> 00:17:51.457
And so I've helped lead
this family planning effort

00:17:51.457 --> 00:17:53.257
because there are 200 million women

00:17:53.257 --> 00:17:55.507
asking us for contraceptives,

00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:59.157
and we need to educate
women about contraceptives

00:17:59.157 --> 00:18:01.167
and their bodies if they
don't know about them.

00:18:01.167 --> 00:18:04.117
And if they do, make sure
they have access to them

00:18:04.117 --> 00:18:08.187
so they can voluntarily decide
if they want to use them

00:18:08.187 --> 00:18:09.910
and if and when to have children.

00:18:11.457 --> 00:18:12.797
- And where do you think this is going?

00:18:12.797 --> 00:18:15.088
I mean, you've talked about
a lot of the good work

00:18:15.088 --> 00:18:16.818
that's already happened, but it also is

00:18:16.818 --> 00:18:18.333
kind of painting a picture.

00:18:18.333 --> 00:18:20.157
There's a lot of important
work that needs to be done.

00:18:20.157 --> 00:18:22.107
What do you see for yourself,

00:18:22.107 --> 00:18:24.337
or I would say for the broader sector,

00:18:24.337 --> 00:18:25.927
what needs to be the priorities

00:18:25.927 --> 00:18:27.107
for the next five or 10 years?

00:18:27.107 --> 00:18:30.537
And what is an outcome we can
be hopeful about in 10 years

00:18:30.537 --> 00:18:32.557
and maybe one that we should be afraid of

00:18:32.557 --> 00:18:34.040
if we don't take action?

00:18:35.477 --> 00:18:37.197
- Yes, well, I will have to be frank

00:18:37.197 --> 00:18:41.647
that this COVID-19 pandemic
has set us back enormously

00:18:41.647 --> 00:18:43.507
in global health and global development.

00:18:43.507 --> 00:18:46.527
You know, where poverty
was on the decline,

00:18:46.527 --> 00:18:48.677
we're now seeing poverty on the rise.

00:18:48.677 --> 00:18:52.867
Where we were making huge
inroads on contraceptives,

00:18:52.867 --> 00:18:56.467
for instance, they're now
going to be 15 million

00:18:56.467 --> 00:18:59.707
unintended pregnancies this
year alone, we estimate,

00:18:59.707 --> 00:19:02.037
because of the lack of contraceptives.

00:19:02.037 --> 00:19:04.467
So the global health
community will have a lot

00:19:04.467 --> 00:19:06.307
of building back to do to make sure

00:19:06.307 --> 00:19:08.237
that vaccination rates stay up,

00:19:08.237 --> 00:19:11.477
to make sure children
are fed and kept alive.

00:19:11.477 --> 00:19:13.407
But some of the things
we can be hopeful about,

00:19:13.407 --> 00:19:15.557
I think do come from these new tools.

00:19:15.557 --> 00:19:17.227
The fact that so many people

00:19:17.227 --> 00:19:20.307
around the world have a
cell phone in their hands.

00:19:20.307 --> 00:19:22.057
You know, information is power.

00:19:22.057 --> 00:19:23.717
Money is power.

00:19:23.717 --> 00:19:26.937
We can give them life-saving
information on their phone.

00:19:26.937 --> 00:19:29.657
We can start to hook them
up in their local community

00:19:29.657 --> 00:19:33.177
with telemedicine, especially
if the health services

00:19:33.177 --> 00:19:34.967
just aren't there or
aren't functioning well,

00:19:34.967 --> 00:19:37.407
which is true in many
countries around the world.

00:19:37.407 --> 00:19:40.027
And so I think these new digital tools

00:19:40.027 --> 00:19:41.247
give us opportunities.

00:19:41.247 --> 00:19:45.707
As long as we get them out
equitably into women's hands,

00:19:45.707 --> 00:19:47.757
we teach women about the internet

00:19:47.757 --> 00:19:50.017
and we make sure that they have access.

00:19:50.017 --> 00:19:53.167
And so it means we have
to do special programming

00:19:53.167 --> 00:19:57.557
just for women so that they do
actually get these new tools,

00:19:57.557 --> 00:19:59.347
or if there's a new seed available

00:19:59.347 --> 00:20:01.777
that's drought resistant or pest resistant

00:20:01.777 --> 00:20:04.837
against climate change, we
have to go the extra mile

00:20:04.837 --> 00:20:07.517
to not just get it in the hands of men,

00:20:07.517 --> 00:20:09.287
but to get it in the hands of women, too,

00:20:09.287 --> 00:20:11.017
So they can get a bigger yield

00:20:11.017 --> 00:20:13.407
and get more income off their farm

00:20:13.407 --> 00:20:14.807
because most women in the world,

00:20:14.807 --> 00:20:17.860
their work is in the informal
sector and they're farmers.

00:20:19.527 --> 00:20:21.937
- And you touched on that
in that last statement.

00:20:21.937 --> 00:20:23.107
What do you see as the role?

00:20:23.107 --> 00:20:24.757
Obviously, there's a
role for policy makers,

00:20:24.757 --> 00:20:26.567
there's a role for philanthropy.

00:20:26.567 --> 00:20:28.727
There's a huge role for
women, obviously, in this.

00:20:28.727 --> 00:20:31.937
What do you see as, how
do we change the culture

00:20:31.937 --> 00:20:33.777
or what's going on in the men's minds?

00:20:33.777 --> 00:20:35.917
And, I'm curious about a lot of places

00:20:35.917 --> 00:20:37.457
we've been talking about where even things

00:20:37.457 --> 00:20:39.757
like family planning, you
can't take for granted.

00:20:39.757 --> 00:20:41.690
But then after that, I
have a few questions about

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:44.550
how do we do it in a lot
of our lives as well?

00:20:45.797 --> 00:20:49.317
- Well, I think to change
culture you really have

00:20:49.317 --> 00:20:51.787
to start where people are

00:20:51.787 --> 00:20:53.037
and you have to listen to them

00:20:53.037 --> 00:20:54.967
about why they have certain beliefs,

00:20:54.967 --> 00:20:57.277
why they do things the way they do them.

00:20:57.277 --> 00:21:02.267
And only over time with a
lot of trust and discussion,

00:21:02.267 --> 00:21:04.597
can you start to introduce new ideas,

00:21:04.597 --> 00:21:06.837
and then people have to
decide for themselves

00:21:06.837 --> 00:21:10.037
do they want to take up those new ideas.

00:21:10.037 --> 00:21:13.380
Yes, you can push down
with top down policy.

00:21:14.367 --> 00:21:16.877
For instance, a good child marriage law,

00:21:16.877 --> 00:21:18.637
but communities have to accept that

00:21:18.637 --> 00:21:21.527
and decide they're not going
to do child marriage again.

00:21:21.527 --> 00:21:24.747
Even in the United States,
things that used to be acceptable

00:21:24.747 --> 00:21:27.927
before in my mom's
generation, we don't expect,

00:21:27.927 --> 00:21:30.247
we don't say those are the norms today.

00:21:30.247 --> 00:21:34.947
And so we spread things and
ideas through our networks

00:21:34.947 --> 00:21:37.067
and then we influence one another.

00:21:37.067 --> 00:21:40.017
So for instance, today,
you'd be pretty hard pressed

00:21:40.017 --> 00:21:43.417
to find too many women
smoking during pregnancy.

00:21:43.417 --> 00:21:46.027
And yet when Jackie
Kennedy was the First Lady

00:21:46.027 --> 00:21:48.487
of the United States, there's
pictures of her smoking.

00:21:48.487 --> 00:21:52.286
Well, we came to learn that is
not good for babies or moms,

00:21:52.286 --> 00:21:54.947
but those ideas there
had to be good policy,

00:21:54.947 --> 00:21:56.947
but we had to have good ideas spread.

00:21:56.947 --> 00:22:00.067
That's how you spread new ideas
in all kinds of communities

00:22:00.067 --> 00:22:03.357
about issues for women, about
breaking down men's thinking

00:22:03.357 --> 00:22:06.137
about what women's roles can or should be,

00:22:06.137 --> 00:22:08.837
and even for us about what ideas we have

00:22:08.837 --> 00:22:10.947
about people of color in our own country

00:22:10.947 --> 00:22:12.687
or indigenous people.

00:22:12.687 --> 00:22:15.617
That's how you change hearts
and minds, and it takes time,

00:22:15.617 --> 00:22:16.860
but it can be done.

00:22:18.467 --> 00:22:20.047
- No, and on that,

00:22:20.047 --> 00:22:22.077
you've touched on this in
your own personal life,

00:22:22.077 --> 00:22:25.107
and actually I wanted some
help with with mine as well,

00:22:25.107 --> 00:22:27.587
is, you talk about this notion,
which is absolutely true,

00:22:27.587 --> 00:22:28.887
and we started there that,

00:22:29.757 --> 00:22:31.457
whether we're talking about
a village in Bangladesh,

00:22:31.457 --> 00:22:34.527
or we're talking about a two
income professional family

00:22:34.527 --> 00:22:36.557
in Seattle or Silicon Valley,

00:22:36.557 --> 00:22:41.147
some of this issue of women
are disproportionately taking,

00:22:41.147 --> 00:22:44.447
doing a lot of what has to
happen in the house and at home,

00:22:44.447 --> 00:22:47.367
even when someone hypothetically
is a rheumatologist.

00:22:47.367 --> 00:22:49.427
My wife's a rheumatologist.

00:22:49.427 --> 00:22:52.617
My question is, and it's true.

00:22:52.617 --> 00:22:53.897
My wife's a rheumatologists.

00:22:53.897 --> 00:22:56.997
I obviously spend my time
working on Khan Academy.

00:22:56.997 --> 00:22:59.306
You know, in theory, I would love

00:22:59.306 --> 00:23:03.277
for there not to be this bias
or this uneven playing field,

00:23:03.277 --> 00:23:04.930
but my wife, you know,

00:23:06.766 --> 00:23:09.960
we never argue, but I hear some
families argue about things.

00:23:12.897 --> 00:23:14.787
How much, who does what?

00:23:14.787 --> 00:23:15.927
One thing that I've thought about,

00:23:15.927 --> 00:23:17.757
do we just have unrealistic expectations

00:23:17.757 --> 00:23:20.507
on both men and women that essentially,

00:23:20.507 --> 00:23:22.067
we want to be super parents,

00:23:22.067 --> 00:23:24.187
which could take 90% of your time.

00:23:24.187 --> 00:23:25.477
We want to be super workers,

00:23:25.477 --> 00:23:27.037
which could take 90% of our time.

00:23:27.037 --> 00:23:29.127
And so if you have two people,

00:23:29.127 --> 00:23:31.117
each three jobs that take 90%,

00:23:31.117 --> 00:23:34.687
you're trying to have 270%
with only 200% of time.

00:23:34.687 --> 00:23:37.390
I'm curious how you and
Bill have navigated that.

00:23:38.637 --> 00:23:40.417
- Well, I think in all of our homes,

00:23:40.417 --> 00:23:42.907
we have to look at who does what,

00:23:42.907 --> 00:23:45.557
and we have to say, what are our values?

00:23:45.557 --> 00:23:48.777
And what do we want as
a family and a society.

00:23:48.777 --> 00:23:50.687
And I think sometimes in the United States

00:23:50.687 --> 00:23:55.687
we've gotten it a bit wrong
where we value work so much,

00:23:56.047 --> 00:23:58.157
as opposed to saying, wait
a minute, in our homes

00:23:58.157 --> 00:24:00.617
we all know we care about our families,

00:24:00.617 --> 00:24:03.387
but how do we balance society to make sure

00:24:03.387 --> 00:24:07.137
there are good childcare
options out there,

00:24:07.137 --> 00:24:09.217
good childcare infrastructure?

00:24:09.217 --> 00:24:11.287
How do we make sure in our homes

00:24:11.287 --> 00:24:12.977
we have the tough conversations

00:24:12.977 --> 00:24:15.527
about why do we both men and women,

00:24:15.527 --> 00:24:18.937
myself included, have
gender normative ideas

00:24:18.937 --> 00:24:21.677
about who takes out the
garbage, or who does the dishes,

00:24:21.677 --> 00:24:24.007
or who gets through to go
to their computer first

00:24:24.007 --> 00:24:26.657
after dinner versus helping
the kids with homework?

00:24:26.657 --> 00:24:29.377
We have to actually look
at all those tasks and say,

00:24:29.377 --> 00:24:31.417
how do we redistribute them?

00:24:31.417 --> 00:24:34.267
And are there times in each of our careers

00:24:34.267 --> 00:24:38.267
where maybe the woman's
career goes first for a while

00:24:38.267 --> 00:24:41.367
and the man helps take care of
the kids a bit more at home,

00:24:41.367 --> 00:24:44.567
and maybe then there's a time
where his career goes forward,

00:24:44.567 --> 00:24:47.337
And she takes care of
the kids more at home?

00:24:47.337 --> 00:24:51.127
There are ways to blend and balance this,

00:24:51.127 --> 00:24:54.807
but we do need better policy
making in the United States.

00:24:54.807 --> 00:24:58.357
We need a paid family
medical leave policy.

00:24:58.357 --> 00:25:01.807
We're the only industrialized
nation that doesn't have one.

00:25:01.807 --> 00:25:04.897
Only 20% of our workers
get maternity leave.

00:25:04.897 --> 00:25:06.777
That's insane.

00:25:06.777 --> 00:25:08.897
And so we need to have good policy

00:25:08.897 --> 00:25:11.047
and we need to have honest conversations

00:25:11.047 --> 00:25:13.870
in our homes if we're
going to change things.

00:25:15.657 --> 00:25:16.707
- No, couldn't agree more.

00:25:16.707 --> 00:25:18.840
And I could talk for hours about this,

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.537
because I think it's a
fascinating subject, actually,

00:25:20.537 --> 00:25:22.767
whether it's in the
village or in the city.

00:25:22.767 --> 00:25:26.161
In the time we have left
Melinda, any parting thoughts?

00:25:26.161 --> 00:25:28.467
Especially to those
who have been watching,

00:25:28.467 --> 00:25:30.347
We have students
watching, we have parents,

00:25:30.347 --> 00:25:32.317
we have people of all ages,

00:25:32.317 --> 00:25:35.247
but I also think about
it, especially to the men,

00:25:35.247 --> 00:25:37.387
not even view some of these
things, we call them chores,

00:25:37.387 --> 00:25:38.797
'cause there might be chores at home,

00:25:38.797 --> 00:25:42.157
but actually to your point,
embrace some of these things.

00:25:42.157 --> 00:25:45.307
Some of my best moments that
I'll never regret in my life

00:25:45.307 --> 00:25:50.237
are when I am able to help
with give my kids a bath.

00:25:50.237 --> 00:25:51.717
They're a little older
now, so that'd be awkward,

00:25:51.717 --> 00:25:54.123
but when I wasn't able
to give them a bath,

00:25:54.123 --> 00:25:58.607
or clean the house or whatever else.

00:25:58.607 --> 00:26:01.017
What messages would you
want everyone to take away,

00:26:01.017 --> 00:26:02.967
both from your book or just in general?

00:26:03.937 --> 00:26:05.357
- I would say, we need to look

00:26:05.357 --> 00:26:06.857
at all of our norms in society.

00:26:06.857 --> 00:26:10.837
Whether I've met people in
a remote village in Senegal,

00:26:10.837 --> 00:26:14.327
I've had men tell me who
have taken on more chores

00:26:14.327 --> 00:26:17.680
or made some changes, normative
changes in their family.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.787
And I'll eventually get them alone

00:26:19.787 --> 00:26:21.547
and I'll say, well, why did you do that?

00:26:21.547 --> 00:26:24.517
And they said, because that work is hard

00:26:24.517 --> 00:26:26.067
that my wife was doing.

00:26:26.067 --> 00:26:28.207
My wife was tired.

00:26:28.207 --> 00:26:30.124
When she's happier, I'm happier

00:26:30.124 --> 00:26:32.737
and my whole family is happier.

00:26:32.737 --> 00:26:34.637
And, in the US we kind of have a saying,

00:26:34.637 --> 00:26:36.527
happy wife, happy life.

00:26:36.527 --> 00:26:37.537
I think it's true.

00:26:37.537 --> 00:26:39.787
If men and women are
happier in their homes,

00:26:39.787 --> 00:26:42.357
our children are happier
and more balanced,

00:26:42.357 --> 00:26:44.987
and we can go out and
do what we want to do.

00:26:44.987 --> 00:26:48.327
So what I want to say to
people is it's possible.

00:26:48.327 --> 00:26:51.337
We just have to really
re-look at things and decide

00:26:51.337 --> 00:26:53.287
what is it that we want.

00:26:53.287 --> 00:26:57.307
And what I know is that society
is going to be better off

00:26:57.307 --> 00:27:00.317
when we have women taking
their equal place to men,

00:27:00.317 --> 00:27:03.587
not just in their homes, but at all places

00:27:03.587 --> 00:27:05.567
that decisions are made

00:27:05.567 --> 00:27:09.367
and we all need to work towards equality.

00:27:09.367 --> 00:27:13.877
In the United States where
208 years away from equality.

00:27:13.877 --> 00:27:16.447
We sometimes think, oh, we're 50 or 60.

00:27:16.447 --> 00:27:18.897
No, we're 208 years away.

00:27:18.897 --> 00:27:22.847
So we have to make these
decisions in our home

00:27:22.847 --> 00:27:24.447
if they're going to have ripple effects

00:27:24.447 --> 00:27:26.137
in our community and in our world.

00:27:26.137 --> 00:27:28.037
And I know it's possible.

00:27:28.037 --> 00:27:29.807
It sound like you're doing
it and thinking about it

00:27:29.807 --> 00:27:32.830
in your own home, Sal,
and that's fabulous.

00:27:33.737 --> 00:27:35.927
- No, we talk about it a lot.

00:27:35.927 --> 00:27:38.417
So, it's a real thing, but it's inspiring

00:27:38.417 --> 00:27:39.907
and it's good to know that,

00:27:39.907 --> 00:27:41.447
in some ways it's comforting to know

00:27:41.447 --> 00:27:43.971
that you and Bill deal with
some of the same problems

00:27:43.971 --> 00:27:46.657
that we're trying, some
of the same tensions,

00:27:46.657 --> 00:27:49.767
but I could talk for hours
about this and your book,

00:27:49.767 --> 00:27:50.600
which is incredible.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:51.937
I really encourage people to take a look.

00:27:51.937 --> 00:27:53.687
It's out in paperback today,

00:27:53.687 --> 00:27:57.197
where it talks about, I mean, just a lot

00:27:57.197 --> 00:27:58.827
of really interesting
things that I think everyone

00:27:58.827 --> 00:28:01.007
needs to be informed about men and women.

00:28:01.007 --> 00:28:02.227
Melinda, thank you so much.

00:28:02.227 --> 00:28:04.857
Thank you so much for the years of support

00:28:04.857 --> 00:28:06.097
for what we're doing at Khan Academy

00:28:06.097 --> 00:28:07.437
and frankly changing the lives

00:28:07.437 --> 00:28:09.327
of millions around the
world and setting an example

00:28:09.327 --> 00:28:10.817
with things like the Giving Pledge

00:28:10.817 --> 00:28:12.737
of kind of, I think, a new generation,

00:28:12.737 --> 00:28:15.047
a new type of philanthropy that I think

00:28:15.047 --> 00:28:16.047
is changing the world.

00:28:16.047 --> 00:28:17.737
So thank you so much.

00:28:17.737 --> 00:28:19.567
- Well, thanks for having
me on your home room.

00:28:19.567 --> 00:28:22.747
I wish you'd been in my home
room when I was growing up.

00:28:22.747 --> 00:28:23.920
All the best to you.

00:28:25.637 --> 00:28:27.087
- See you, thank you so much.

00:28:28.657 --> 00:28:30.357
Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:28:30.357 --> 00:28:31.697
That was a wonderful conversation.

00:28:31.697 --> 00:28:36.097
As you could imagine, we could
have spoken for hours more.

00:28:36.097 --> 00:28:37.817
So much in that.

00:28:37.817 --> 00:28:40.737
But I really do encourage
you all to take a look

00:28:40.737 --> 00:28:42.207
at Moment of Lift.

00:28:42.207 --> 00:28:44.857
I was telling Melinda before we went on,

00:28:44.857 --> 00:28:46.617
there's a lot that I expected to see

00:28:46.617 --> 00:28:47.767
that I found very insightful

00:28:47.767 --> 00:28:50.547
about how do you affect global
health and empower women,

00:28:50.547 --> 00:28:52.707
but there's a lot of really,
I would say almost intangible

00:28:52.707 --> 00:28:55.077
and very personal stories about Melinda

00:28:55.077 --> 00:28:57.377
and her journeys and her
struggles, which I think,

00:28:57.377 --> 00:28:59.667
I found a lot in that, as well.

00:28:59.667 --> 00:29:02.197
So definitely take a look at that.

00:29:02.197 --> 00:29:04.697
I'll also let y'all know
we're going to have another

00:29:04.697 --> 00:29:09.077
Homeroom live stream
tomorrow with Jennifer Doudna

00:29:09.077 --> 00:29:13.117
who is a 2020 Nobel Prize
in chemistry winner.

00:29:13.117 --> 00:29:16.927
So we're going to geek out
hard tomorrow in chemistry.

00:29:16.927 --> 00:29:18.647
So encourage you all to come.

00:29:18.647 --> 00:29:20.627
And actually, if you want
do a little bit of research

00:29:20.627 --> 00:29:22.447
ahead of time, if you need a
little background knowledge,

00:29:22.447 --> 00:29:24.627
I know a good website called Khan Academy

00:29:24.627 --> 00:29:26.907
to make sure your your
chemistry is boned up

00:29:26.907 --> 00:29:28.727
to be able to talk with
a Nobel prize winner.

00:29:28.727 --> 00:29:32.470
So look forward to that and
I'll see everyone tomorrow.

00:29:33.581 --> 00:29:36.164
(cheery music)

